ō dynasty of the Ryukyu Kingdom, ruling from 1461 until his death. Ostensibly the son of the prior king Shō Taikyū, he may have taken power through force. He is portrayed as a cruel and violent ruler in the later official histories of the kingdom, forcing ministers into hiding and subduing the island of Kikaijima through a costly and brutal military campaign. He was a patron of the Japanese deity Hachiman, adopting his symbol as a royal crest, taking the divine name "Hachiman-no-Aji", and building the Asato-Hachimangū shrine to him near Naha after his conquests.", "newValue": "Shō Toku (1441–1469) presided over a tumultuous, brief reign as the final monarch of the First Shō dynasty of the Ryukyu Kingdom, from 1461 until his death in 1469. His rule marked a critical juncture in Ryukyuan history, precipitating the dynasty's collapse and the subsequent rise of the more enduring Second Shō dynasty, which would solidify the kingdom's unique maritime trading empire in East Asia. His controversial accession, possibly through force, and his subsequent portrayal as a tyrannical figure in official histories highlight the intense political rivalries and dynastic instability prevalent in the archipelago during the mid-15th century.

During his eight-year reign, Shō Toku engaged in a costly and brutal military campaign to subdue Kikaijima, one of the Amami Islands, in 1466. This expansionist endeavor, while temporarily extending Ryukyuan control, severely depleted the kingdom's resources and fostered discontent among the nobility. Internally, he is depicted as a cruel and violent ruler, allegedly forcing ministers into hiding and consolidating power through oppressive means. His patronage of the Japanese deity Hachiman, adopting his symbol as a royal crest, further illustrates the significant cultural and religious influences from Japan on the Ryukyuan court during this period.

